{
  "_what": "Kestrel Motors, body shop line 1 - a simulated body-in-white line: eight cells in a straight run, 20 welding and vision robots as parts of their cells, a body every 60-75 s, three bodies of buffer between cells. Nothing here is a real plant.",
  "_why_units": "A robot is not a machine on the floor: it makes no body of its own, its cell does. Each robot is a `unit` of its cell, with its own table (FR10_R2), its own state and fault number, and no counters; the cell's state follows its robots - one robot down is the cell down with that robot's fault, one robot away for its tip dress is the cell in setup.",
  "_tip_dress": "Every welding robot dresses its tips every 300 welds, for 8 s, between bodies. A weld takes 1.6 s gun-to-gun, so a robot's cycle is its welds times 1.6 s and always ends inside its cell's. `ops_per_piece` is the spot welds that robot puts on one body, so a robot with 30 welds a body dresses every 10 bodies. `TipWear` on each robot counts the welds since its last dress; `start` staggers them so a cell's robots do not all dress together.",
  "_mf10": "Metal finish is people with sanders and a light tunnel, not robots: `cycle_cv` 0.18 draws every body's cycle around its mean, so MF10 is the slower, noisier cell in the records and the cell whose buffers move.",
  "_qi10": "Gap-and-flush measurement: one vision robot. Its rejects go to the rework bay, not to scrap, so its ScrapCount stays zero; the rework loop is not modelled in this pack.",
  "_faults": "Fault numbers are the PLC's own: 101 weld gun fault, 102 robot collision stop. The tag map's reason_map names them in this plant's downtime vocabulary.",
